#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Background {#background}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Full quantum state tomography of an $N$-qubit system requires exponentially "
"many measurements to estimate all $4^N -1$ independent parameters of the "
"density matrix. For a system of 50 qubits, for example, this process "
"requires on the order of $10^{30}$ shots, which is more than a billion times"
" the number of stars in the observable universe. Shadow tomography "
"[\\[1\\]](#references) is a quantum information protocol that constructs a "
"“classical shadow” of the quantum state, from which target observables can "
"be estimated by using a number of shots that scales logarithmically in the "
"number of observables and is independent of system size $N$, making it "
"relevant across many areas in quantum computing, including energy "
"estimation, entanglement entropy and correlation function calculations, "
"kernel estimation in quantum machine learning, and others.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial shows how to use shadow tomography to estimate local "
"observables, such as the single-qubit magnetization $\\langle Z_i \\rangle$ "
"for qubit $i$, the two-point correlation function $\\langle Z_i "
"Z_j\\rangle$, and the two-qubit reduced density matrix (2-RDM), of the "
"highly entangled Greenberger-Horne-Zeilinger (GHZ) state. The tutorial first"
" illustrates a small-scale shadow tomography workflow on a 10-qubit GHZ "
"state by using statevector simulation. Then it shows a 100-qubit "
"demonstration on real quantum hardware.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shadow tomography protocol {#shadow-tomography-protocol}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The shadow tomography protocol has the following steps:\n"
"1. Apply a random unitary $U$, drawn from a unitary ensemble, to the initial quantum state.\n"
"2. Measure the outcome in the computational basis, obtaining a bitstring $\\ket{b}$.\n"
"3. Store the classical snapshot, $\\hat{\\rho} = \\mathcal{M}^{-1}(U^{\\dagger}\\ket{b}\\bra{b}U)$, where $\\mathcal{M}^{-1}$ is the inverse of the measurement channel $\\mathcal{M}(\\rho)=\\mathbb{E}[U^{\\dagger}\\ket{b}\\bra{b}U]$.\n"
"4. Repeat the process $T$ times, where the collection of $T$ snapshots $\\{\\hat{\\rho}_t\\}$ forms a classical shadow $S(\\rho, T)$.\n"
"5. Estimate observable $o$ of operator $O$ as $\\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{t=1}^{T}\\text{Tr}(O\\hat{\\rho}_t)$.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Shadow tomography variants {#shadow-tomography-variants}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The choice of the unitary ensemble, that is, basis, in step 1 of the "
"protocol, affects the sample complexity, variance of estimates, and "
"information completeness of a given shadow tomography. Many ensemble groups "
"have been considered in the literature, each with trade-offs between "
"complexity and observable estimation efficiency. The properties of the most "
"commonly used variants are summarized in the following table, although this "
"list is not exhaustive.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"| Variant | Ensemble | Sample complexity | Circuit depth | Suitable observables |\n"
"| ------------ | ---------------------------------| ---------| ---| -------- |\n"
"| local Pauli [\\[1\\]](#references) | single-qubit Paulis $\\{X, Y, Z\\}$ | $\\mathcal{O}(3^k\\text{log}M/\\varepsilon^2)$ | 1 | local/few-body |\n"
"| global Clifford [\\[1\\]](#references) | $N$-qubit Clifford |$\\mathcal{O}(\\text{Tr}(O^2)\\text{log}M/\\varepsilon^2)$ | $\\mathcal{O}(N\\text{log}N)$ | low-rank, fidelity |\n"
"| fermionic Gaussian [\\[2\\]](#references), [\\[3\\]](#references)| matchgate circuits | $\\mathcal{O}(\\binom{\\mu}{k}\\frac{1}{\\varepsilon^2})$  | $\\mathcal{O}(N^2)$ | fermionic |\n"
"| shallow shadows [\\[4\\]](#references), [\\[5\\]](#references) | low-depth circuits| $\\mathcal{O}(k2^k\\text{log}M/\\varepsilon^2)$ | $\\mathcal{O}(\\text{log}N)$ | non-local (hardware-friendly) |\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The terms used in the table are defined as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\"sample complexity\" refers to the number of measurements needed for a "
"given shadow tomography variant.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\"circuit depth\" describes how expensive the measurements are to implement "
"on quantum hardware.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$N$ is the number of qubits.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"$k$ is the locality of the observable (for example, $\\langle Z_i \\rangle$ "
"has $k=1$ and $\\langle Z_i Z_j\\rangle$ has $k=2$).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$M$ is the number of observables to be estimated simultaneously.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$\\varepsilon$ is the additive error tolerance.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$\\mu$ is the number of fermions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Though the local Pauli ensemble is the most hardware-friendly, it pays a "
"$3^k$ sample complexity penalty, which makes it ill-suited for non-local "
"property estimation. More generally, the local-shadow sample-complexity "
"bound scales as $4^k$, improving to $3^k$ in the special case of tensor-"
"product observables.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Conversely, while the global Clifford ensemble can be used to estimate non-"
"local observables, its circuit depth is not favorable. Fermionic Gaussian "
"shadows offer utility in simulations of many-body physics, chemistry, and "
"materials, but require matchgate circuits, which might carry significant "
"overhead on general purpose gate-based devices.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Shallow shadows has a circuit depth between the local Pauli and global "
"Clifford, making it a highly promising variant for practical implementation,"
" though there are still some open questions regarding optimal circuit depth "
"for a given observable.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"As the frontier of shadow tomography research continues expanding, some of "
"the most relevant problems to address include optimally choosing the "
"measurement ensemble for a target set of observables, improving sample "
"complexity bounds, and extending the protocol to time-evolved observables. "
"Nonetheless, shadow tomography still presents one of the leading workflows "
"for extracting maximal classical information from a quantum state using a "
"minimum number of measurements, which makes it ideal for characterizing "
"quantum states at scale where both full tomography and classical simulation "
"are impossible.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "The local Pauli ensemble {#the-local-pauli-ensemble}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial uses the random Pauli basis, that is, single-qubit Clifford "
"gates, to collect shadows and estimate properties of the GHZ state. Note "
"that the Pauli measurements are a great choice for estimating local "
"observables (such as spin density, local correlation functions, and position"
" probability density), but not global ones (such as total energy, momentum, "
"and charge).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Here, a random Pauli basis $\\{X, Y, Z\\}$ per qubit is independently chosen"
" and the corresponding single-qubit rotation is applied, followed by "
"measurement:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{equation}\n"
"U = U_0 \\otimes U_1 \\otimes \\cdots \\otimes U_{n-1}, U_q \\in \\{I, H, S^{\\dagger}H\\}\n"
"\\end{equation}\n"
"\\tag{1}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "For this ensemble, the measurement channel's inverse is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"\\begin{equation}\n"
"\\hat{\\rho}_q=3U_q^{\\dagger}\\ket{b_q}\\bra{b_q}U_q - I\n"
"\\end{equation}\n"
"\\tag{2}\n"
"$$\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The factor of $3$ comes from averaging over the three Pauli bases, which "
"produces a depolarizing channel with parameter $\\frac{1}{3}$. The full "
"estimator then factorizes as a product of single-qubit estimators. As shown "
"in the table, for $M$ observables, each of locality $k$, the number of Pauli"
" shadows needed to estimate all observables to additive error $\\varepsilon$"
" with high probability is "
"$\\mathcal{O}(\\frac{3^k\\text{log}(M)}{\\varepsilon^2})$. The system size "
"does not affect the measurement cost as long as the observables remain "
"local, so exponentially many observables can be estimated simultaneously "
"with only logarithmic cost.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Median of means {#median-of-means}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A plain mean over $T$ snapshots reduces the variance of a single-shot estimate of an observable as $\\frac{1}{T}$, but is sensitive to outliers. Median of means can be used to remedy this. The process involves the following:\n"
"- Splitting the $T$ snapshots into $K$ equally-sized groups\n"
"- Computing the mean within each group\n"
"- Returning the median across group means\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This tutorial performs local Pauli shadow tomography to estimate properties "
"of the GHZ state, first using a 10-qubit simulated example and then "
"demonstrating a 100-qubit hardware experiment.\n"
msgstr ""

